ML_PAGE("l5-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l5\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 5 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 5 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 48 multiple-choice questions (8 of them coding questions) sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them. Every question also has a <b>harder version</b> on the same concept, one reveal below its answer.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>48</strong><span>MCQs in the notes, each with a harder version</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>8</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Bloom challenge questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l5\" data-quiz=\"5\">Start Lecture 5 simulator</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"5\" data-quiz-mode=\"hard\">Hard mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"5\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Bloom challenge</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l5\" id=\"l5p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">23 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 5 - MCQ Exam Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s0\" title=\"Lecture 5 - MCQ Exam Preparation\">MCQ overview</button></li><li data-full=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s1\" title=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Know instantly</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part A - Feature Scaling MCQs</li><li data-full=\"2. Feature scaling fundamentals\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s3\" title=\"2. Feature scaling fundamentals\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Scaling fundamentals</button></li><li data-full=\"3. StandardScaler\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s4\" title=\"3. StandardScaler\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>StandardScaler</button></li><li data-full=\"4. MinMaxScaler\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s5\" title=\"4. MinMaxScaler\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>MinMaxScaler</button></li><li data-full=\"5. RobustScaler\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s6\" title=\"5. RobustScaler\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>RobustScaler</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part B - Feature Selection MCQs</li><li data-full=\"6. Feature-selection fundamentals\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s8\" title=\"6. Feature-selection fundamentals\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Selection fundamentals</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Filter methods\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s9\" title=\"7. Filter methods\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Filter methods</button></li><li data-full=\"8. VarianceThreshold\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s10\" title=\"8. VarianceThreshold\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>VarianceThreshold</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Statistical feature-selection methods\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s11\" title=\"9. Statistical feature-selection methods\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Statistical tests</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Wrapper methods\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s12\" title=\"10. Wrapper methods\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Wrapper methods</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Embedded methods\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s13\" title=\"11. Embedded methods\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Embedded methods</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Data leakage\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s14\" title=\"12. Data leakage\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Data leakage</button></li><li data-full=\"Part C - Coding Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s15\" title=\"Part C - Coding Questions\">Coding questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part D - Answer Keys\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s16\" title=\"Part D - Answer Keys\">Answer keys</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Feature Scaling answers (Q1-Q20)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s17\" title=\"13. Feature Scaling answers (Q1-Q20)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Answers Q1-Q20</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Feature Selection answers (Q21-Q40)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s18\" title=\"14. Feature Selection answers (Q21-Q40)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Answers Q21-Q40</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Coding answers (Q41-Q48)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s19\" title=\"15. Coding answers (Q41-Q48)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Answers Q41-Q48</button></li><li data-full=\"Part E - The traps that distinguish difficult MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s20\" title=\"Part E - The traps that distinguish difficult MCQs\">Six exam traps</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">Part F - What I would memorize before the exam</li><li data-full=\"16. The three scaling formulas\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s22\" title=\"16. The three scaling formulas\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Scaling formulas</button></li><li data-full=\"17. The three feature-selection families\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s23\" title=\"17. The three feature-selection families\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Selection families</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Final rapid-fire revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s24\" title=\"18. Final rapid-fire revision\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Rapid-fire revision</button></li><li data-full=\"19. How to prepare for both MCQ and subjective questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s25\" title=\"19. How to prepare for both MCQ and subjective questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>How to revise</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l5\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 5 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>23 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 5 - MCQ Exam Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Feature Scaling and Feature Selection</h4>\n<p>For Lecture 5, I would focus on understanding which technique to use, why it works, and what happens when it is applied incorrectly.</p>\n<p>The chapter has two major sections:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Feature Scaling: StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, RobustScaler, outliers and scale-sensitive algorithms.</li>\n<li>Feature Selection: Filter, Wrapper and Embedded methods, VarianceThreshold, statistical tests, RFE, Random Forest feature importance and data leakage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture also includes a practical comparison using the Wisconsin Breast Cancer dataset, where 15 selected features achieve a reported accuracy of 95.26%, compared with 95.09% using all 30 features. This is an important example of why more features do not necessarily produce better models.</p>\n<p>We'll cover 40 conceptual and scenario MCQs, followed by 8 coding questions, with answer keys and explanations afterward.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What you should know instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Immediate meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Scaling</td>\n<td>Change numerical magnitude/range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature Selection</td>\n<td>Keep useful features, remove unnecessary ones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>StandardScaler</td>\n<td>Mean 0, standard deviation 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MinMaxScaler</td>\n<td>Maps training minimum to 0, maximum to 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RobustScaler</td>\n<td>Median and IQR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Z-score</td>\n<td>Distance from mean in SD units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQR</td>\n<td>$Q_3-Q_1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KNN/SVM</td>\n<td>Highly scale-sensitive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision Tree/Random Forest</td>\n<td>Generally scale-insensitive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Curse of dimensionality</td>\n<td>Problems arising in high-dimensional feature spaces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter methods</td>\n<td>Statistical scores or thresholds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrapper methods</td>\n<td>Repeated model fitting and feature elimination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Embedded methods</td>\n<td>Importance/selection during model training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VarianceThreshold</td>\n<td>Removes features with insufficient variance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chi-Square</td>\n<td>Statistical association with categorical target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ANOVA F-test</td>\n<td>Differences across target classes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutual Information</td>\n<td>Statistical dependence between feature and target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SelectKBest</td>\n<td>Keeps top $K$ scored features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RFE</td>\n<td>Recursive Feature Elimination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest importance</td>\n<td>Importance derived from fitted trees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data leakage</td>\n<td>Test/validation information influences training</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are the chapter's central topics, especially the three scalers and three feature-selection families.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5p-d2\">Part A - Feature Scaling MCQs</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Feature scaling fundamentals</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>What is the primary purpose of feature scaling?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Remove irrelevant variables</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Transform numerical feature magnitudes to more comparable scales</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Increase the number of observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Convert labels into categories</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H1</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A KNN model uses age (20-70) and annual income (RM20,000-RM500,000). A colleague rescales both features to comparable ranges. Which statement best describes what the colleague has and has <strong>not</strong> done?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They have removed the less useful feature, so the model now has fewer inputs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They have proven that age and income are equally important to the target.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They have changed the numerical magnitudes so that units no longer decide the distance, without removing any feature or judging its relevance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They have increased the number of observations so KNN has more neighbours.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Feature scaling <strong>transforms values onto comparable scales</strong>: both columns remain and nothing about their predictive relevance is decided. A describes feature selection. B confuses scale with importance - scaling only removes the arbitrary advantage of large units. D changes rows, which scaling never does.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Which statement best differentiates feature scaling from feature selection?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scaling removes columns while selection changes units</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both always remove features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling transforms feature values, whereas selection retains a subset of features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Selection must always be performed before scaling</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H2</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A pipeline has 50 numeric columns. Step 1 applies StandardScaler; step 2 applies SelectKBest(k = 12). Which description of the data after each step is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> After step 1: 50 columns with changed values; after step 2: 12 of the original columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> After step 1: 12 columns; after step 2: 12 standardized columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> After step 1: 50 columns unchanged; after step 2: 12 new principal components.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> After step 1: fewer columns because constant ones are dropped; after step 2: 50 columns.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Scaling changes <strong>values</strong> but keeps every column (50); selection keeps a <strong>subset</strong> of the original columns (12). B swaps the roles of the two steps. C confuses selection with extraction (PCA creates new axes). D: StandardScaler never drops columns - that is VarianceThreshold's job.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>A dataset contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age      = 25\nIncome   = 85,000\nTenure   = 3\n</code></pre>\n<p>A KNN model uses Euclidean distance.</p>\n<p>What is the primary concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Age will always dominate distance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Income may dominate because of its larger numerical scale</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> KNN cannot process numerical data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The features must be converted to text</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H3</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Customer A is (age 25, income 85,000, tenure 3) and customer B is (age 35, income 86,000, tenure 6). Using raw Euclidean distance, roughly what share of the <strong>squared</strong> distance comes from income?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 33%, because there are three features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> About 50%, because income and age differ the most</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About 1%, because the income gap is only 1,000 out of 85,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About 99.99%, because 1,000² = 1,000,000 dwarfs 10² = 100 and 3² = 9</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Squared differences are 100 (age), 1,000,000 (income) and 9 (tenure); income contributes $1{,}000{,}000 / 1{,}000{,}109 \\approx 99.99\\%$. A assumes equal shares. B ignores the squaring of large units. C reasons with the <em>relative</em> change, but Euclidean distance uses the <strong>absolute</strong> difference - the core of distance dominance.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Which algorithm is generally the least dependent on feature scaling?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KNN</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RBF SVM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> K-Means</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Random Forest</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H4</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which pair of models would both behave essentially the same whether or not the income column is multiplied by 1,000?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KNN and K-Means</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A decision tree and a random forest</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An RBF SVM and PCA</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Logistic regression with regularization and a neural network</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Trees split one feature at a time on a <strong>threshold</strong>; multiplying income by 1,000 just multiplies the threshold, giving the same partitions - and a forest is made of trees. A and C rely on distances or variance, so scale changes them. D is optimized by gradients and regularization penalties that depend on feature scale.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Why is feature scaling especially important for KNN?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KNN uses distances between observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> KNN requires all features to be categorical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> KNN removes outliers automatically</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> KNN uses Gini impurity</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H5</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For KNN, which statement explains <strong>why</strong> scaling can change its predictions, not just its speed?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scaling changes which stored observations count as \"nearest\", because the neighbours are chosen by a distance that mixes all features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Scaling removes outliers, so KNN sees cleaner data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling converts numeric features into categories, which KNN prefers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Scaling reduces the number of neighbours K.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>KNN predicts from its K nearest neighbours; rescaling a feature reshapes the distances and can therefore <strong>change the neighbour set</strong> and the vote (the 3D widget's nearest neighbour changes from A to B). B: no scaler removes outliers. C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. StandardScaler</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Remember:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}}$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>What does StandardScaler use to transform numerical features?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Minimum and maximum</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mean and standard deviation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Median and IQR</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Correlation and covariance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H6</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A feature has training mean 40 and standard deviation 8. After StandardScaler is fitted on the training data, a <strong>test</strong> value of 40 arrives. What is its scaled value, and why?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It depends on the test set's own mean, so it cannot be known in advance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1, because 40 equals the mean.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0, because the test value is transformed with the training mean and standard deviation: $(40-40)/8=0$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 5, because $40/8=5$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>StandardScaler stores the <strong>training</strong> μ and σ and reuses them: $z=(40-40)/8=0$. A describes refitting on the test data, which is leakage/inconsistency. B confuses \"at the mean\" with z = 1. D forgets to subtract the mean.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>After applying StandardScaler to a nonconstant training feature, its mean and standard deviation are approximately:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mean 1, SD 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mean 0, SD 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Mean 0, SD 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Mean 1, SD 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H7</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>After StandardScaler is fitted on a training feature, which statement is guaranteed?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Every transformed value lies between -1 and 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The transformed <strong>training</strong> feature has mean ≈ 0 and standard deviation ≈ 1, but individual values (and test values) can be far outside ±1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The transformed test feature also has mean exactly 0 and standard deviation exactly 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The transformed feature follows a normal distribution.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Standardizing only fixes the <strong>training</strong> mean and SD. A is false - z = -2 or +5 are possible. C: the test set is transformed with training statistics, so its own mean and SD are only approximately 0 and 1. D: standardizing shifts and stretches; it does not change the shape of the distribution.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>A student's score is 70. The class mean is 50 and standard deviation is 10.</p>\n<p>What is the Z-score?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 7</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - StandardScaler</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=\\frac{70-50}{10}=\\boxed{2}$$</div>\n<p>Meaning the score is two standard deviations above the mean.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H8</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Student P has a z-score of +1.2 in Maths (mean 60, SD 5). Student Q scored 74 in Physics (mean 70, SD 2). Who did <strong>relatively</strong> better, and what was P's raw score?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> P did better; P's raw score was 61.2.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Q did better; P's raw score was 72.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They did equally well; both are above their class mean.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Q did better; P's raw score was 66, while Q's z-score is 2.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>P's raw score is $60+1.2\\times5=66$. Q's z-score is $(74-70)/2=2$ - two standard deviations above the mean against P's 1.2 - so Q did relatively better. A adds 1.2 marks instead of 1.2 standard deviations. B multiplies the mean by 1.2 instead of using the SD. C ignores how far above the mean each student is.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A standardized observation has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=-1.5$$</div>\n<p>What does this mean?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is 1.5 units above the mean</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is 1.5 standard deviations below the mean</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The original value was negative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The observation must be removed</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H9</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A transaction amount has training mean RM200 and standard deviation RM50. A new transaction scales to $z=-1.5$. What was the amount, and what does the sign tell you?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM125 - the negative sign means it is 1.5 standard deviations <strong>below</strong> the mean, not that the amount is negative.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> -RM75 - a negative z-score means a refund.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM275 - it is 1.5 standard deviations above the mean.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM198.50 - it is 1.5 ringgit below the mean.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$x=\\mu+z\\sigma=200-1.5\\times50=125$. A negative z-score simply means \"below the mean\". B confuses the sign of z with the sign of the original value. C flips the direction. D treats z as raw units instead of standard deviations.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>What is one limitation of StandardScaler?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It cannot produce negative transformed values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It always maps data between 0 and 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Its mean and standard deviation can be influenced by extreme outliers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It automatically eliminates irrelevant features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H10</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Salaries are mostly near RM50,000, but one executive earns RM10 million. Which statement about StandardScaler on this column is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> StandardScaler ignores the executive because it uses the median.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The executive is removed during fit().</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The outlier inflates both the mean and the standard deviation, so ordinary salaries get squeezed into a narrow band of z-scores.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All salaries are mapped into [0, 1], so the outlier becomes harmless.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Mean and SD are <strong>sensitive to extreme values</strong>: the large SD shrinks every ordinary salary's z-score towards a narrow band, while the executive stays far out. A describes RobustScaler. B: no scaler deletes data. D describes MinMaxScaler - and even there the outlier would compress everyone else.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. MinMaxScaler</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Recall:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=\\frac{x-x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}}}$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>MinMaxScaler normally transforms the observed training range to:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -1 to 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0 to 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0 to 100</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Mean 0 and standard deviation 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H11</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A MinMaxScaler is fitted on training values with minimum 20 and maximum 60. Which set of outputs is correct for the inputs 20, 40 and 60?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0, 0.5, 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> -1, 0, 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.33, 0.67, 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 20, 40, 60, because MinMax only applies to test data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$x'=\\frac{x-20}{60-20}$: 20 → 0, 40 → 0.5, 60 → 1 - the <strong>training</strong> range becomes [0, 1]. B is the shape of a [-1, 1] scaling. C divides by the maximum instead of the range. D is wrong: fit_transform scales the training data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x=30,\\quad x_{\\min}=10,\\quad x_{\\max}=50$$</div>\n<p>What is the Min-Max scaled value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.75</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1.00</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Working - MinMaxScaler</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{30-10}{50-10}\n=\\frac{20}{40}\n=\\boxed{0.5}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H12</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A MinMaxScaler learned $x_{\\min}=20$ and $x_{\\max}=60$ from training data. What are the scaled values of a training value 50 and a new test value 70?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.83 and 1.17</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.75 and 1.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.75 and 1 (clipped)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.5 and 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$(50-20)/40=0.75$ and $(70-20)/40=1.25$. Without clipping, values beyond the training range leave [0, 1]. A divides by $x_{\\max}$ instead of the range. C assumes clipping, which is not the default. D miscalculates both.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Why is MinMaxScaler particularly sensitive to extreme outliers?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because it uses median</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because it uses standard deviation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because extreme minimum/maximum values determine the scaling range</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because it removes all high-valued observations</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H13</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Training values are [10, 12, 14, 16, 1000]. After MinMaxScaler, roughly where does 16 land, and what is the cause?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 0.6 - values are spread evenly by rank.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> About 0.5 - 16 is the middle value of the range 10-1000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About 0.006 - the single value 1000 sets the maximum, so all ordinary values are squashed near 0.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Exactly 1 - the largest ordinary value becomes 1.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$(16-10)/(1000-10)\\approx0.006$. MinMax uses the <strong>extremes</strong> as its reference points, so one outlier compresses everything else. A describes rank/quantile behaviour. B confuses the position with the midpoint of the range. D would only hold without the outlier.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>A MinMaxScaler was fitted using training values ranging from 10 to 100.</p>\n<p>A new test observation has value 120. Using default scaling without clipping, what happens?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is automatically deleted</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It can be transformed to a value greater than 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The scaler must automatically refit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is always transformed to exactly 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H14</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A MinMaxScaler was fitted on training values 10-100. A test value of 5 arrives, and later a value of 145. What happens with default settings?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both are clipped to 0 and 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The scaler refits on the test data so both fit inside [0, 1].</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They become about -0.056 and 1.5 - outside [0, 1], because the training minimum and maximum are reused.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both values are deleted as outliers.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$(5-10)/90\\approx-0.056$ and $(145-10)/90=1.5$. The [0, 1] guarantee applies only to the <strong>training</strong> range; clipping is off by default. B would be leakage and would make train and test inconsistent. A and D describe behaviour MinMaxScaler does not have by default.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A video dataset contains view counts:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[1,5,25,300,1295,19000]$$</div>\n<p>What is the most likely consequence of Min-Max scaling?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All values become negative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> All values become identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The ordinary observations become compressed near zero due to the very large maximum</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The 19,000-view observation disappears</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H15</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For the lecture's views [1, 5, 25, 300, 1295, 19000], what share of the [0, 1] Min-Max range do the <strong>five ordinary videos</strong> occupy together?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 6.8% - they all lie between 0 and 0.068.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> About 83% - five of the six videos are ordinary.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About 50% - Min-Max spreads values evenly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0%, because they are all mapped to exactly 0.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The largest ordinary video, 1,295, maps to $(1295-1)/(19000-1)\\approx0.068$, so all five sit in the bottom 6.8% of the range while the viral video takes 1. B counts videos, not scaled positions. C ignores the outlier. D is too extreme: they are compressed, not identical (5 → 0.0002, 300 → 0.0157).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>This is the same type of extreme-value example used in the lecture's comparison of the three scaling methods.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. RobustScaler</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_scalers\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Three scalers, one outlier</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Animated · The lecture&#x27;s video views under all three scalers. Make the viral video bigger and watch which scaler lets it squash the ordinary videos.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Recall:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=\\frac{x-\\text{Median}}{IQR}}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$IQR=Q_3-Q_1$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Which statistics are used by RobustScaler?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mean and standard deviation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Minimum and maximum</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Median and interquartile range</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Variance and correlation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H16</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Training values are [2, 4, 6, 8, 100]. RobustScaler uses the median and the IQR (linear-interpolated quartiles $Q_1=4$, $Q_3=8$). What does 100 become?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1 - RobustScaler maps the maximum to 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 23.5 - $(100-6)/(8-4)$; it is still an extreme value after robust scaling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.96 - $(100-4)/100$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0 - outliers are set to the median.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Median = 6, IQR = 8 - 4 = 4, so $(100-6)/4=23.5$. The outlier <strong>remains extreme</strong>; robust scaling only stops it from choosing the reference statistics. A describes MinMaxScaler. C mixes the formulas. D: no scaler replaces outliers.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>A dataset contains salaries ranging from RM2,000 to RM10 million, with a few extremely high-income individuals.</p>\n<p>Which scaler is most suitable to consider when trying to reduce the influence of those outliers on scaling parameters?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> MinMaxScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> StandardScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RobustScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> LabelEncoder</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H17</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Income ranges from RM2,000 to RM10 million, with a handful of genuine billionaire-level customers. Which recommendation is best?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> MinMaxScaler, because bounded [0, 1] values are always safest.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Delete every customer above RM1 million, then use MinMaxScaler.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> StandardScaler, because its mean ignores outliers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RobustScaler, because the median and IQR are hardly affected by the extreme customers - while recognising that those customers still have very large scaled values.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>RobustScaler's reference statistics <strong>resist</strong> the extreme incomes, so ordinary customers keep a readable spread; the outliers are kept, not removed. A: one extreme maximum would compress everyone else. B throws away genuine observations, which is a data decision, not a scaling technique. C is false - the mean is pulled by outliers.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Median=20,\\quad Q_1=10,\\quad Q_3=30$$</div>\n<p>What is the robust-scaled value of $x=25$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.50</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><strong>Working - RobustScaler</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$IQR=30-10=20$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{25-20}{20}=\\boxed{0.25}$$</div>\n<p>These three formulas are worth knowing for any calculation-based MCQs.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H18</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>RobustScaler learned $Q_1=40$, median $=55$ and $Q_3=70$. What are the scaled values of 100 and 25?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.5 and -1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1 and 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.45 and -0.3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.5 and -0.5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>IQR = 70 - 40 = 30. $(100-55)/30=1.5$ and $(25-55)/30=-1$. The output is not bounded to [0, 1] and can be negative. C divides by 100. D uses the wrong IQR. B looks like MinMax-style clipping.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Which statement about RobustScaler is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It deletes all outliers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It makes all transformed values lie between 0 and 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It uses statistics less sensitive to outliers, but the outliers remain in the dataset</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It selects the most important features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H19</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>After RobustScaler, a fraud-detection team says \"the outliers are gone, so we no longer need to think about them\". What is wrong with this claim?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - RobustScaler removes values beyond 1.5 x IQR.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RobustScaler clips everything into [0, 1].</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RobustScaler only selects the most informative features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RobustScaler keeps every outlier - it only uses outlier-resistant statistics for centring and scaling, so the extreme transactions are still extreme.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Robust scaling <strong>resists</strong> outliers when computing the median and IQR but does not delete, clip or fix them; the 19,000-view video still scales to about 18. A confuses scaling with outlier filtering. B describes clipping/MinMax. C is feature selection.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> StandardScaler is always better than RobustScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RobustScaler guarantees the best test accuracy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> MinMaxScaler automatically corrects data-entry errors</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The appropriate scaler depends on the distribution, algorithm and evaluation results</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H20</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Three teams claim the \"best\" scaler for the same dataset. Which claim is the most defensible?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"StandardScaler is always best because it is the most common.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"RobustScaler is always best because real data has outliers.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"We compared the scalers inside cross-validation for our KNN model and RobustScaler gave the best validation accuracy on this skewed feature.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"MinMaxScaler is best because bounded values cannot overfit.\"</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The right scaler depends on the <strong>distribution, the algorithm and evaluation results</strong> - C is the only claim backed by validation on this problem. A and B over-generalize. D is false: scaling does not prevent overfitting.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5p-d7\">Part B - Feature Selection MCQs</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Feature-selection fundamentals</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Which best describes feature selection?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Converting all features into binary variables</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Selecting informative features while removing unnecessary or redundant features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Increasing dataset size</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Scaling numerical values into the same range</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H21</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which change is <strong>feature selection</strong> rather than feature engineering, scaling or extraction?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Replacing Debt and Income with Debt ÷ Income</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Standardizing all numeric columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Replacing 20 correlated sensors with 3 principal components</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dropping StudentID and ShoeSize but keeping Attendance and GPA</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Selection <strong>keeps a subset of the original columns</strong>. A creates a new feature (engineering). B rescales values. C builds new synthetic axes (extraction/PCA).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>A dataset contains 10,000 features, many of which are irrelevant or redundant.</p>\n<p>What is a possible consequence?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It guarantees higher accuracy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It eliminates overfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It can increase computational cost, overfitting risk and dimensionality problems</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It automatically converts the model to supervised learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H22</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A model trained on 10,000 mostly irrelevant features has 99% training accuracy and 70% test accuracy. Which explanation is most consistent with Lecture 5?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Too many noisy dimensions let the model fit training-specific noise; the data is sparse relative to the space, so it generalizes poorly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The features need StandardScaler, which would fix the gap.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> More features always increase accuracy, so the test set must be wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The model is underfitting because it has too few features.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Irrelevant and redundant dimensions increase <strong>overfitting risk</strong> and sparsity (the curse of dimensionality); the train-test gap is the symptom. B: scaling does not remove irrelevant features. C contradicts \"more features ≠ better\". D: 99% training accuracy is not underfitting.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>Which correctly identifies the three main feature-selection families?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scaling, Normalization, Standardization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Filter, Wrapper, Embedded</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regression, Classification, Clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Supervised, Unsupervised, Reinforcement</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H23</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Match each technique to its family: (1) VarianceThreshold, (2) RFE with Logistic Regression, (3) Random Forest feature_importances_.</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> (1) wrapper, (2) filter, (3) embedded</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> (1) filter, (2) wrapper, (3) embedded</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> (1) embedded, (2) wrapper, (3) filter</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> (1) filter, (2) embedded, (3) wrapper</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>VarianceThreshold uses a statistic without fitting a model (filter); RFE repeatedly refits a model and eliminates features (wrapper); forest importance comes out of the model's own training (embedded). The other options shuffle the families.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Filter methods</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture introduces threshold-based filtering, variance filtering and statistical scoring as major filter-based approaches.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>What is the main characteristic of a filter method?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It repeatedly trains the final estimator with every possible feature subset</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It selects features using statistical measures or predefined criteria, without repeatedly fitting the final estimator</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It always uses a neural network</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It requires Random Forest</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H24</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which statement about filter methods is <strong>false</strong>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They can be applied before choosing the final model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They are usually much cheaper than wrapper methods.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Univariate filters can miss features that are only useful in combination.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They select features by repeatedly retraining the final model on different subsets.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Repeated retraining on subsets is what <strong>wrapper</strong> methods do. Filters score features with statistics or thresholds, independent of the final model (A), cheaply (B), but often one feature at a time, so interactions can be missed (C).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>A text vectorizer is configured with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">min_df=0.10\nmax_df=0.85\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does this mean?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Retain only terms appearing in exactly 10% of documents</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Remove terms appearing in fewer than 10% or more than 85% of documents</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Remove 85% of all words randomly</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Select the top 10 terms</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H25</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A corpus has 1,000 documents. With <code>min_df=0.10, max_df=0.85</code>, which terms survive?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A term in 50 documents and a term in 900 documents</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only terms in exactly 100 to 850 documents that are also among the top 10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A term in 120 documents and a term in 800 documents</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every term, because thresholds only apply to test data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>min_df = 0.10 means at least 100 documents; max_df = 0.85 means at most 850. 120 and 800 are inside the band; 50 is too rare and 900 too common (A). B invents a top-10 rule. D: the thresholds are applied when the vocabulary is learned from the training documents.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>What does max_features=2000 mean in a CountVectorizer?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The dataset must contain exactly 2,000 rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model must make 2,000 predictions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Limit the generated vocabulary to at most 2,000 features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Remove every term appearing 2,000 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H26</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>After <code>min_df</code> and <code>max_df</code> filtering, 5,300 terms remain. The vectorizer also has <code>max_features=2000</code>. What is the resulting vocabulary?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5,300 terms, because max_features only applies when there are fewer than 2,000 terms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2,000 randomly chosen terms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 7,300 terms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> At most 2,000 terms - the most frequent of those that passed the document-frequency filters</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><code>max_features</code> <strong>caps</strong> the vocabulary, keeping the top terms by frequency among those allowed. A misreads the cap. B: the choice is by frequency, not random. C adds the numbers instead of capping.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. VarianceThreshold</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_variance\"><div class=\"vis-title\">VarianceThreshold and its blind spot</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Slide the variance threshold over the lecture&#x27;s genetic markers - then reveal which feature actually predicts fraud.</figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>A feature has the same value for every training observation.</p>\n<p>What is its variance?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Cannot be determined</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H27</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A column has training values [5, 5, 5, 5, 6]. What is its variance, and does <code>VarianceThreshold(threshold=0)</code> keep it?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Variance 0; it is removed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Variance 0.16; it is kept, because only exactly-zero variance is removed at threshold 0.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Variance 1; it is kept.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Variance 0.2; it is removed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Mean 5.2; squared deviations 0.04 x 4 + 0.64 = 0.8; population variance 0.8/5 = 0.16 &gt; 0, so it survives. A ignores the single 6. C and D miscalculate. A near-constant column survives a zero threshold - you would need a higher threshold to drop it.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>Which of the following is correct about VarianceThreshold?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It requires target labels for every observation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It selects features based on correlation with the target</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It can operate without knowing the target labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It always removes all binary features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H28</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Why can VarianceThreshold be applied to an unlabelled dataset while SelectKBest(chi2) cannot?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> VarianceThreshold only looks at the variation of each column in X; chi-square scores each feature <strong>against y</strong>, so it needs labels.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> VarianceThreshold needs fewer rows.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Chi-square only works on images.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> VarianceThreshold is a wrapper method, so it trains its own labels.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>VarianceThreshold is <strong>unsupervised</strong> - it never touches the target - whereas chi-square measures association with the target, so <code>fit</code> needs <code>y</code>. B and C are false. D: VarianceThreshold is a filter and creates no labels.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A fraud dataset contains a rare binary indicator that is 1 for only 1% of transactions, but that indicator may be strongly associated with fraud.</p>\n<p>What is a possible weakness of VarianceThreshold?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It may remove a potentially useful low-variance feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It guarantees that rare features are retained</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It automatically detects fraud</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It converts the target to a continuous number</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H29</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A binary fraud flag is 1 in 1% of rows (variance $p(1-p)$). With <code>VarianceThreshold(threshold=0.01)</code>, what happens to it?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is kept, because 1% equals the threshold.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is kept, because VarianceThreshold protects binary features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is removed (variance 0.0099 &lt; 0.01), even if it is the strongest fraud signal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is converted into a continuous feature.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$0.01\\times0.99=0.0099$, which is below 0.01, so the flag is dropped. VarianceThreshold judges <strong>variation, not relevance</strong>. A confuses the share of ones with the variance. B and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Statistical feature-selection methods</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>Which method is used in the lecture to evaluate association between non-negative features and a categorical target?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> StandardScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Chi-Square</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> MinMaxScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RobustScaler</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H30</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A team applies <code>SelectKBest(chi2)</code> to features that include negative values (for example standardized income). What is the most accurate statement?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Chi-square handles negative values by taking their absolute value automatically.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> scikit-learn's chi2 requires non-negative inputs, so this raises an error; use a non-negative scaling (MinMaxScaler) or a different score such as f_classif.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Chi-square requires a continuous target, so the problem is the labels.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Chi-square only works after RFE.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture notes this compatibility trap: <code>chi2</code> needs <strong>non-negative</strong> features and a categorical target. A is false. C reverses the requirement (chi-square is for categorical targets). D is unrelated.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>ANOVA F-test for classification is mainly useful for:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Comparing how a continuous feature varies across target classes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Converting categories to one-hot vectors</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Measuring numerical range only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Calculating KNN distances</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H31</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For a binary target (Default / No default), which feature would the ANOVA F-test score highest?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A feature whose class means differ a lot relative to the spread within each class</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A feature with the largest numerical range</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A feature with the most missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A feature whose values are identical in both classes but very spread out</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>ANOVA compares <strong>between-class</strong> variation with <strong>within-class</strong> variation; large mean differences relative to within-class spread give a high F score. B confuses range with separation. C is irrelevant. D has no difference between classes, so F is near zero.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>What is the central idea behind mutual information?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Measure whether a feature has the largest numerical value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Measure statistical dependence between a feature and the target</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Remove every feature with negative values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Calculate the mean of every column</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H32</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A feature X relates to the target only in a U-shape (both very low and very high X mean fraud). Which score is most likely to detect this?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pearson correlation between X and y</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mutual information, because it measures general statistical dependence, including nonlinear patterns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> VarianceThreshold</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The feature's maximum value</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Mutual information asks how much knowing X <strong>reduces uncertainty</strong> about y, which can capture nonlinear dependence; a symmetric U-shape can give near-zero linear correlation (A). C ignores y. D says nothing about the relationship.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>What is the main purpose of SelectKBest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Randomly select K observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Automatically train K different algorithms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Retain the K highest-scoring features according to a specified scoring function</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Create K new polynomial features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H33</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>SelectKBest(f_classif, k=5)</code> is fitted on training data with 20 features. Which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It keeps the 5 rows with the highest scores.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It trains 5 different models and keeps the best.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It keeps a random 5 of the 20 features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It scores each of the 20 features against y with the ANOVA F-test and keeps the 5 with the highest scores.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>SelectKBest ranks <strong>features</strong> with the chosen scoring function and retains the top k. A confuses features with rows. B describes model selection. C: selection is by score, not random.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>In the lecture's Wisconsin Breast Cancer example, selecting 15 out of 30 features produced the following reported results:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature count</th>\n<th>Accuracy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>95.09%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>95.26%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>What is the most justified conclusion?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> More features always worsen model performance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature selection can reduce dimensionality without necessarily reducing predictive performance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The 15-feature model is guaranteed to outperform all future models</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Feature selection eliminates the need for test data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H34</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A study reports 95.09% accuracy with 30 features and 95.26% with 15 chi-square-selected features, where the features were selected on the whole dataset before 5-fold cross-validation. Which evaluation is best?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The 15-feature model is clearly superior and should replace the 30-feature model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The result proves that half of any dataset's features are useless.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Feature selection always reduces accuracy, so the result must be wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The smaller model is not worse and is cheaper, but the 0.17-point gain is tiny and the selection leaked information across folds, so it does not prove better generalization.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The result supports \"more features ≠ better\" but the difference is within normal noise, and selecting before cross-validation <strong>leaks</strong> fold information. A over-interprets 0.17 points. B over-generalizes from one dataset. C contradicts the data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The reported 30-to-15 feature comparison is presented on slides 42-45.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Wrapper methods</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l5_rfe\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Recursive feature elimination</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Animated · Recursive feature elimination on the Wisconsin data, one refit and one dropped feature at a time, then compared with chi-square.</figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>Which technique is an example of a wrapper method?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> StandardScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> VarianceThreshold</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> MinMaxScaler</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H35</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which procedure is a <strong>wrapper</strong> method?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Keep the 15 features with the highest chi-square scores.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Drop features whose variance is below 0.1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Train a model, drop the feature it ranks lowest, retrain on the rest, and repeat until 10 remain.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Keep the features with the largest random-forest importances from one fit.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Repeatedly <strong>refitting a model</strong> to evaluate subsets is the wrapper idea (RFE). A and B are filters. D uses importance from a single training run, which is embedded selection.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q36</span><div><p>How does RFE select features?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It removes a random set of columns once</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It repeatedly trains an estimator and eliminates lower-ranked features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It removes only features with missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It converts continuous features into categories</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H36</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>RFE (step = 1) starts with features A, B, C, D, E and must keep 3. After the first fit the model ranks them E &gt; C &gt; A &gt; D &gt; B. After refitting on A, C, D and E it ranks C &gt; E &gt; D &gt; A. Which features remain?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> C, E and A</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> E, C and D</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A, B and C</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> C, E and D</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Round 1 drops the weakest, B. Round 2 refits on A, C, D, E and drops the new weakest, <strong>A</strong> - even though A outranked D in round 1. C, E and D remain. A assumes the first ranking is kept. B never refits. RFE re-ranks after every elimination; that is what makes it model- and subset-dependent.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q37</span><div><p>What is a major limitation of RFE?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It cannot be used for classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It requires every feature to have zero variance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It can be computationally expensive because it repeatedly fits an estimator</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It always retains all features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H37</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>RFE with <code>step=1</code> reduces 10,000 text features to 100. Roughly how many times is the estimator fitted, and what does the lecture recommend instead?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 9,900 fits - use a fast filter (document-frequency thresholds, chi-square) first, then RFE on a smaller candidate set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Exactly 1 fit - RFE trains once.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About 100 fits - one per selected feature.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About 10,000 x 100 fits - so RFE can never be used on text.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Each round removes one feature, so going from 10,000 to 100 takes about 9,900 refits - the <strong>computational cost</strong> that makes wrappers slow. The lecture's advice: filter first. B and C misunderstand the loop. D exaggerates; RFE is fine on a pre-filtered set (and <code>step</code> can be larger).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Embedded methods</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q38</span><div><p>Which algorithm is used in the lecture as an example of model-based embedded feature selection?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Random Forest</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> MinMaxScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CountVectorizer</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> VarianceThreshold</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H38</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Why is Random Forest importance classed as an <strong>embedded</strong> method rather than a wrapper?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because it uses statistics without any model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because the importance comes out of a single training run of the model itself, instead of refitting the model on many feature subsets.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because it only works for regression.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because it removes features automatically during prediction.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Embedded methods get selection information <strong>during the model's own fitting</strong> (impurity reductions in the trees). Wrappers refit repeatedly. A describes filters. C is false. D: importances rank features; you still choose a cutoff.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q39</span><div><p>A Random Forest assigns a high feature-importance score to customer age.</p>\n<p>Which conclusion is justified?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Age definitely causes the outcome</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Age was important to the fitted model's tree-based decisions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Removing age will always improve accuracy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Age must have the highest numerical range</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H39</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A forest ranks <code>worst radius</code> and <code>worst perimeter</code> as two of its top features. Then you drop <code>worst radius</code>, retrain, and accuracy barely changes while <code>worst perimeter</code>'s importance rises. What is the best explanation?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The forest was wrong about worst radius.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Importance measures causation, so worst radius never mattered.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The two features are strongly correlated; importance was shared between them, and the remaining twin took over.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Removing a feature always increases accuracy.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Impurity importance is <strong>shared among correlated features</strong>; when one is removed, its twin carries the same information. Importance says how the fitted model used a feature, not whether it causes the outcome (B) or whether it is irreplaceable. A misreads the result. D over-generalizes.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Data leakage</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q40</span><div><p>Which workflow best prevents data leakage?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fit scaling and feature selection on the complete dataset, then split into training and test sets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fit preprocessing separately on training and test data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Split the dataset first, learn transformations from training data, and reuse them on test data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Select features using test-set accuracy before training</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H40</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A team does: (1) MinMaxScaler.fit on all data, (2) split into train/test, (3) SelectKBest.fit on train, (4) train and evaluate. What is wrong?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - only the selector needs to be fitted on train.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Step 3 should use the test set so the features match it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The order is fine, but the model should be trained on test data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Step 1 leaks: the scaler's min and max were learned from test rows, so the evaluation is not on truly unseen data.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Every</strong> learned preprocessing step - scaling and selection - must be fitted after splitting, on training data only. A ignores the scaler leak. B and C are both forms of fitting on the test set.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The lecture closes by emphasizing that scaling and feature selection must not learn from the held-out test data.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part C - Coding Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These focus on understanding what code does, not memorizing entire programs. The techniques follow the scikit-learn examples in the lecture.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q41</span><div><p><strong>Coding - StandardScaler</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nscaler = StandardScaler()\n\nX_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)\nX_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why is transform() used instead of fit_transform() for X_test?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Test data cannot contain numerical values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Test data must use scaling statistics learned from the training data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> fit_transform() always removes features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It makes the model unsupervised</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Reuse training scaling parameters on test data.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H41</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Compare two versions of the last line: <code>X_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)</code> versus <code>X_test_scaled = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X_test)</code>. What is the practical consequence of the second version?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Better test accuracy, because the test data is scaled more precisely.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> No difference, because both produce mean 0 and SD 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The test data is put on its own scale, inconsistent with the scale the model learned, and the test statistics have influenced preprocessing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It raises an error because fit_transform cannot be used twice.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The model learned relationships on the <strong>training</strong> scale; a separately fitted scaler shifts test values differently (and uses test information). A is the trap - any \"improvement\" is not a fair estimate. B ignores that the two transformations use different μ and σ. D: it runs without error, which is what makes the bug dangerous.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q42</span><div><p><strong>Coding - MinMaxScaler</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler\n\nscaler = MinMaxScaler()\n\nX = [[10], [20], [30]]\n\nX_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the output for the value 20?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>20 lies halfway between 10 and 30, producing 0.5.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H42</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">scaler = MinMaxScaler()\nX = [[10], [20], [30], [50]]\nX_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the output for 20, and what would <code>scaler.transform([[60]])</code> return?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.5 and 1.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.25 and 1.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.4 and 1.2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.25 and 1.0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>min 10, max 50: $(20-10)/40=0.25$ and $(60-10)/40=1.25$ (no clipping by default). A uses the range of the first three values. C divides by the maximum. D wrongly clips the new value.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q43</span><div><p><strong>Coding - VarianceThreshold</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import VarianceThreshold\n\nX = [\n    [1, 2],\n    [1, 5],\n    [1, 8]\n]\n\nselector = VarianceThreshold(threshold=0)\nX_new = selector.fit_transform(X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What happens?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both columns are removed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The first column is removed because it has zero variance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The second column is removed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both columns are retained</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The first column is constant, giving variance 0.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H43</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">X = [[1, 2, 0],\n     [1, 5, 1],\n     [1, 8, 0],\n     [1, 11, 1]]\nselector = VarianceThreshold(threshold=0.5)\nX_new = selector.fit_transform(X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>How many columns does <code>X_new</code> have?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1 - only the second column (variance 11.25) exceeds 0.5; the first has variance 0 and the third 0.25.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2 - the constant column is the only one removed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 3 - VarianceThreshold never removes binary columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0 - every column is below 0.5.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Variances: column 1 = 0; column 2: mean 6.5, squared deviations 20.25, 2.25, 2.25, 20.25 → 11.25; column 3 (0, 1, 0, 1) = 0.25. Only 11.25 &gt; 0.5. B forgets that the binary column (0.25) is also below the threshold. C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q44</span><div><p><strong>Coding - CountVectorizer thresholds</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\n\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer(\n    min_df=0.10,\n    max_df=0.85,\n    max_features=2000\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the main purpose of this configuration?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scale numerical data between 0 and 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reduce vocabulary size by filtering very rare and very common terms and limiting the number of features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Perform Random Forest classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Compute Euclidean distance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Filter by document frequencies and cap vocabulary size.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H44</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A spam filter uses <code>CountVectorizer(min_df=0.10, max_df=0.85, max_features=2000)</code>. A rare phrase, \"verify_unusual_transfer\", appears in 2% of emails but almost only in spam. What happens, and what is the lesson?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is kept because it is predictive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is kept because max_features allows up to 2,000 terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is promoted to the top of the vocabulary.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is removed by min_df (2% &lt; 10%) - frequency thresholds ignore predictive value, so rare-but-useful terms can be lost.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Threshold filtering uses <strong>document frequency only</strong>; 2% fails min_df = 0.10 regardless of its link to spam. A assumes the filter looks at the target. B: max_features applies after the frequency filters. C is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q45</span><div><p><strong>Coding - SelectKBest</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest, chi2\n\nselector = SelectKBest(\n    score_func=chi2,\n    k=15\n)\n\nX_selected = selector.fit_transform(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Assume that X_train contains 30 non-negative features.</p>\n<p>What is the resulting number of features?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 30</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 45</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 15</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Depends only on the number of rows</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>k=15 retains 15 selected features.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H45</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>X_train</code> has 30 non-negative features. It goes through <code>SelectKBest(chi2, k=15)</code> and then <code>RFE(LogisticRegression(), n_features_to_select=5)</code>. How many features does the final model see, and which families were used?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 15 - the RFE step cannot reduce further</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 5 - a filter first (chi-square) and then a wrapper (RFE) on the smaller set</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 20 - the two selections are added together</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 30 - selection steps only rank features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The filter keeps 15; RFE then recursively eliminates down to 5 - exactly the \"filter first, then wrapper\" workflow the lecture recommends for efficiency. A, C and D misunderstand how chained selectors work.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q46</span><div><p><strong>Coding - RFE</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_selection import RFE\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\nmodel = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)\n\nselector = RFE(\n    estimator=model,\n    n_features_to_select=5,\n    step=1\n)\n\nselector.fit(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It retains 5 observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It repeatedly removes one lower-ranked feature until 5 features remain</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It retains all features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It calculates Z-scores</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>RFE removes one lower-ranked feature per iteration until five remain.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H46</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>RFE(estimator=LogisticRegression(), n_features_to_select=5, step=1)</code> is fitted on 12 features. What does <code>selector.ranking_</code> contain?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Five features with rank 1 (kept) and the others ranked 2-8 in reverse order of elimination - the last one removed gets 2.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Twelve ranks 1-12 ordered by chi-square score</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Rank 1 for the first five columns in the dataset</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only the five kept features; the others are deleted from ranking_</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>RFE gives rank 1 to the survivors; the seven eliminated features get ranks 2...8, with <strong>higher rank = eliminated earlier</strong> (the last removed is 2). B mixes in chi-square. C and D misdescribe the attribute - every original feature gets a rank.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q47</span><div><p><strong>Coding - Random Forest importance</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n\nmodel = RandomForestClassifier(random_state=42)\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nimportance = model.feature_importances_\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does importance represent?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Predicted probabilities for each test sample</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Training accuracy for each observation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Estimated importance scores for the input features based on the fitted Random Forest</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Missing-value percentages</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Returns model-based feature-importance scores.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H47</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>After <code>model.fit(X_train, y_train)</code>, <code>model.feature_importances_</code> for a Random Forest sums to 1 across features. Which use of it is <strong>valid</strong>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Prove that the top feature causes the target.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Report each customer's predicted probability.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Rank features by how much the fitted trees used them to reduce impurity, then choose a cutoff to build a smaller feature set and validate it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Replace cross-validation, because importance already measures generalization.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Importances are <strong>model-based scores of how much each feature contributed to the forest's splits</strong>; to select, you pick a threshold and then validate. A confuses prediction with causation. B is predict_proba. D: importance is computed on training data and says nothing about generalization.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q48</span><div><p><strong>Coding - Spot the preprocessing mistake</strong></p>\n<p>Consider:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest, chi2\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\npipeline = Pipeline([\n    (&quot;scaler&quot;, StandardScaler()),\n    (&quot;selector&quot;, SelectKBest(chi2, k=10)),\n    (&quot;model&quot;, LogisticRegression())\n])\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is a potential technical problem?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Logistic Regression can never be used with feature selection</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> StandardScaler may produce negative values, but chi2 requires non-negative features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Pipeline cannot contain more than two steps</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Chi-Square automatically removes all target labels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>StandardScaler can produce negative values, which are incompatible with chi2.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H48</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which pipeline is both <strong>leak-free</strong> and <strong>technically valid</strong> for chi-square selection?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>Pipeline([(\"scaler\", StandardScaler()), (\"selector\", SelectKBest(chi2, k=10)), (\"model\", LogisticRegression())])</code> fitted on X_train</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>SelectKBest(chi2, k=10).fit(X, y)</code> on all data, then a pipeline of MinMaxScaler and LogisticRegression on X_train</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>Pipeline([(\"scaler\", MinMaxScaler()), (\"selector\", SelectKBest(chi2, k=10)), (\"model\", LogisticRegression())])</code> fitted on X_train</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A MinMaxScaler fitted on X_test and a selector fitted on X_train</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>MinMaxScaler keeps the training features non-negative (as chi2 requires), and fitting the whole pipeline on X_train keeps every learned step away from the test data. A fails because StandardScaler produces negative values for chi2. B leaks through the selector. D fits a scaler on test data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>This is a particularly useful implementation trap: the overall idea of combining scaling, selection and modelling is valid, but individual transformations must also be compatible with each other.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part D - Answer Keys</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Feature Scaling answers (Q1-Q20)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>D</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Important calculations</h3>\n<p><strong>Q8 - StandardScaler</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=\\frac{70-50}{10}=\\boxed{2}$$</div>\n<p>Meaning the score is two standard deviations above the mean.</p>\n<p><strong>Q12 - MinMaxScaler</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{30-10}{50-10}\n=\\frac{20}{40}\n=\\boxed{0.5}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Q18 - RobustScaler</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$IQR=30-10=20$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\\frac{25-20}{20}=\\boxed{0.25}$$</div>\n<p>These three formulas are worth knowing for any calculation-based MCQs.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Feature Selection answers (Q21-Q40)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>31</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>32</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>23</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>33</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>34</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>35</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>26</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>36</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>27</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>37</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>28</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>38</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>39</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Coding answers (Q41-Q48)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>41</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>Reuse training scaling parameters on test data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>42</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>20 lies halfway between 10 and 30, producing 0.5.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>43</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>The first column is constant, giving variance 0.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>44</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>Filter by document frequencies and cap vocabulary size.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>45</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>k=15 retains 15 selected features.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>46</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>RFE removes one lower-ranked feature per iteration until five remain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>47</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>Returns model-based feature-importance scores.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>48</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>StandardScaler can produce negative values, which are incompatible with chi2.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part E - The traps that distinguish difficult MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Trap 1 - Scaling does not equal feature selection</h4>\n<p>If a dataset has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100\\ features$$</div>\n<p>and you apply StandardScaler, it normally still has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100\\ features$$</div>\n<p>Only the feature values have changed.</p>\n<p>If you apply SelectKBest with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$k=20$$</div>\n<p>the result has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$20\\ features$$</div>\n<p>That is feature selection.</p>\n<h4>Trap 2 - RobustScaler doesn't remove outliers</h4>\n<p>A common misleading answer choice is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>RobustScaler eliminates extreme values.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>It uses median and IQR, which are less sensitive to outliers.</p>\n<p>The outliers remain in the dataset, potentially with very large transformed values.</p>\n<h4>Trap 3 - Low variance does not automatically mean useless</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Feature A: 50% zeros, 50% ones\nFeature B: 99% zeros, 1% ones\n</code></pre>\n<p>Feature B has lower variance.</p>\n<p>But perhaps its rare positive value strongly indicates a fraud transaction.</p>\n<p>A purely variance-based filter does not know that.</p>\n<p>This is the difference between:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Feature variation}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Predictive relevance}$$</div>\n<p>VarianceThreshold primarily evaluates the first.</p>\n<p>Supervised statistical tests such as Chi-Square evaluate associations with the target.</p>\n<h4>Trap 4 - Chi-Square and RFE are not interchangeable</h4>\n<p>The lecture reports that Chi-Square and RFE retained only eight common features out of their respective sets of fifteen.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Chi-Square evaluates individual feature associations with the target.</p>\n<p>RFE repeatedly fits a predictive model and removes lower-ranked features based on that estimator.</p>\n<p>The methods use different definitions of usefulness, so they can select different subsets.</p>\n<p>Neither method automatically guarantees the best final model.</p>\n<h4>Trap 5 - Feature importance is not causality</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Importance(Age)=0.4$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Importance(Income)=0.2$$</div>\n<p>This means the fitted model assigned more importance to Age under that particular importance measure.</p>\n<p>It does not establish:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Age causes the target outcome.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A feature may be correlated with a hidden variable, redundant with another feature, or useful for prediction without being causally responsible.</p>\n<h4>Trap 6 - Data leakage can happen during feature selection too</h4>\n<p>Students often remember:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Don't scale using test data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But forget:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Don't select features using test data either.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">selector.fit(X, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>before splitting into training and test sets can leak information about the future test observations into feature selection.</p>\n<p>The safest practical approach is to put the scaling, selection and estimator into a pipeline and fit the pipeline only on the training data.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5p-d21\">Part F - What I would memorize before the exam</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. The three scaling formulas</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>StandardScaler</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}}$$</div>\n<p>Mean and standard deviation.</p>\n<h4>MinMaxScaler</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=\\frac{x-x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}}}$$</div>\n<p>Minimum and maximum.</p>\n<h4>RobustScaler</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{x'=\\frac{x-\\text{Median}}{Q_3-Q_1}}$$</div>\n<p>Median and interquartile range.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. The three feature-selection families</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Main mechanism</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter</td>\n<td>Use statistical criteria/thresholds</td>\n<td>VarianceThreshold, Chi-Square</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrapper</td>\n<td>Repeatedly fit a model using feature subsets</td>\n<td>RFE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Embedded</td>\n<td>Derive selection information from model training</td>\n<td>Random Forest importance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Final rapid-fire revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Most appropriate answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Numerical features have very different scales</td>\n<td>Feature Scaling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mean 0 and SD 1</td>\n<td>StandardScaler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training range transformed to 0-1</td>\n<td>MinMaxScaler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extreme outliers distort scaling statistics</td>\n<td>RobustScaler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main purpose is to remove unnecessary columns</td>\n<td>Feature Selection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Too many noisy dimensions</td>\n<td>Curse of dimensionality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remove constant features</td>\n<td>VarianceThreshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Select terms by document frequencies</td>\n<td>Threshold-based Filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Statistical association with categorical target</td>\n<td>Chi-Square</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare continuous features across target classes</td>\n<td>ANOVA F-test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detect statistical dependencies</td>\n<td>Mutual Information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep highest 15 feature scores</td>\n<td>SelectKBest(k=15)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeatedly eliminate lower-ranked features</td>\n<td>RFE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature importance from fitted decision-tree ensemble</td>\n<td>Random Forest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeated model fitting makes selection expensive</td>\n<td>Wrapper method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature selection learns from test labels</td>\n<td>Data Leakage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A feature has high predictive importance</td>\n<td>Does not prove causality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use StandardScaler before chi2</td>\n<td>Potential non-negative input error</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. How to prepare for both MCQ and subjective questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>I would prioritize your revision in this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>StandardScaler vs MinMaxScaler vs RobustScaler: know the formulas, uses, outlier behavior and what transformed numbers mean.</li>\n<li>Filter vs Wrapper vs Embedded: know their mechanisms, representative algorithms and computational trade-offs.</li>\n<li>VarianceThreshold vs supervised statistical tests: understand why variance is not the same thing as predictive relevance.</li>\n<li>RFE and Random Forest feature importance: understand how each relies on fitted models.</li>\n<li>Data leakage: know the correct train/test pipeline and be able to identify incorrect code.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If time is limited, don't spend it memorizing every scikit-learn argument. Make sure you can look at a dataset, identify the actual problem, choose an appropriate technique, and explain why the alternatives are less suitable.</p>\n<p>The two ideas holding this chapter together are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Scaling fixes differences in numerical magnitude.}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Selection reduces unnecessary feature dimensions.}}$$</div>\n<p>And both only become useful when their effect is checked through proper evaluation on genuinely unseen data.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l5-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Bloom challenge - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry, coding, hackathon, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the lecture&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"5\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Lecture 5 Bloom challenge</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"5\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which set of formulas matches the three scalers in the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Standard: $\\frac{x-x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}}$; MinMax: $\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$; Robust: $\\frac{x-\\mu}{IQR}$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Standard: $\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$; MinMax: $\\frac{x-x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}}$; Robust: $\\frac{x-\\text{median}}{IQR}$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Standard: $\\frac{x-\\text{median}}{\\sigma}$; MinMax: $\\frac{x}{x_{\\max}}$; Robust: $\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Standard: $\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma^2}$; MinMax: $\\frac{x-x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max}}$; Robust: $\\frac{x-Q_1}{Q_3}$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Mean and standard deviation</strong> (Standard), <strong>minimum and maximum</strong> (MinMax), <strong>median and IQR</strong> (Robust). A swaps Standard and MinMax and gives Robust the mean. C and D mix up the reference statistics; D divides by the variance and uses $x_{\\max}$ alone as the range.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which list contains <strong>only filter</strong> methods?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RFE, chi-square, VarianceThreshold</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Random Forest importance, ANOVA F-test</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> VarianceThreshold, chi-square, ANOVA F-test, mutual information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RFE, Random Forest importance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Filters score features with statistics or thresholds, without repeatedly training the final model. A includes RFE (a wrapper). B includes forest importance (embedded). D is one wrapper and one embedded method.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>According to the lecture, which group of models is generally <strong>not</strong> sensitive to feature scaling?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KNN, K-Means and PCA</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SVM and regularized regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Neural networks and logistic regression trained by gradient descent</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Decision Tree, Random Forest and tree-based boosting such as XGBoost / LightGBM</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Tree models split on <strong>thresholds within one feature at a time</strong>, so a consistent rescaling moves the threshold without changing the partition. A uses distances or variance. B uses margins and penalties. C is affected through gradient-based training.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why are RobustScaler's reference statistics hardly moved when one value of 19,000 is added to a column of ordinary values?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The median and IQR depend on the <strong>middle</strong> of the sorted data, so an extreme value only shifts the positions slightly instead of pulling the statistics towards itself.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RobustScaler deletes values beyond 1.5 x IQR before fitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RobustScaler caps every value at 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The IQR is computed from the minimum and maximum.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The mean and SD (and the min and max) are pulled by the size of an extreme value; <strong>order statistics</strong> like the median and quartiles only care about where values sit in the ranking. B and C describe behaviour RobustScaler doesn't have. D describes MinMax's statistics, which are the most outlier-sensitive.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Feature selection throws information away. Why can it still <strong>improve</strong> test accuracy?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fewer features always mean a higher training accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Dropping irrelevant or redundant features removes noise the model could overfit to and reduces the sparsity problems of high-dimensional spaces, so the model generalizes better.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Selection rescales the remaining features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Selection adds the removed features' information to the ones that remain.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Irrelevant dimensions give a flexible model more chances to fit <strong>training-specific noise</strong> (overfitting) and spread the data thinly (curse of dimensionality). A is false - training accuracy usually falls slightly. C confuses selection with scaling. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>On the Wisconsin data, chi-square's top 15 and RFE's top 15 overlap only partly. What is the most basic reason?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One of the two methods must be wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They use different random seeds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Chi-square scores each feature <strong>on its own</strong> against the target, while RFE judges features <strong>inside a fitted model</strong> alongside the others, so redundancy and interactions are handled differently.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RFE ignores the target variable.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Univariate filters can rank several near-duplicate features highly because each one, alone, is predictive; a model-based wrapper can drop duplicates whose information is already covered. Different selection criteria give different subsets, and neither is automatically \"right\" (A). B and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>StandardScaler (population standard deviation, as scikit-learn uses) is fitted on [2, 4, 6, 8]. What is the scaled value of 8?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.342</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.162</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 3</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$\\mu=5$; $\\sigma=\\sqrt{(9+1+1+9)/4}=\\sqrt5\\approx2.236$; $z=(8-5)/2.236\\approx1.342$. B uses the sample SD ($\\sqrt{20/3}\\approx2.582$). C divides by 2. D forgets to divide.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>For the views [1, 5, 25, 300, 1295, 19000], scikit-learn's RobustScaler uses median 162.5 and linear-interpolated quartiles $Q_1=10$, $Q_3=1046.25$. What does 19,000 become?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 14.7</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 18.18</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.068</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$IQR=1046.25-10=1036.25$; $(19000-162.5)/1036.25\\approx18.18$. The viral video is still a clear outlier - robust scaling <strong>does not remove</strong> outliers. A is MinMax's value for the maximum. D is MinMax's value for 1,295. B is an arithmetic slip.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>You want VarianceThreshold to drop binary (0/1) features that take the same value in more than 80% of rows. Which threshold should you use?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.8</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.64</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.16</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A binary feature with share $p$ of ones has variance $p(1-p)$; at $p=0.8$ that is $0.8\\times0.2=0.16$, and anything more imbalanced has lower variance. A and B use the share instead of the variance. C squares 0.8. Remember the lecture's caveat: a rare flag may still be very predictive.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>The views [1, 5, 25, 300, 1295, 19000] were scaled three ways. Match each output column to its scaler.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Column</th>\n<th>Outputs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>X</td>\n<td>0, 0.0002, 0.0013, 0.016, 0.068, 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>-0.49, -0.49, -0.49, -0.45, -0.31, 2.23</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Z</td>\n<td>-0.16, -0.15, -0.13, 0.13, 1.09, 18.18</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> X = Standard, Y = MinMax, Z = Robust</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> X = MinMax, Y = Robust, Z = Standard</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> X = MinMax, Y = Standard, Z = Robust</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> X = Robust, Y = Standard, Z = MinMax</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>X is exactly within [0, 1] with the maximum at 1 (<strong>MinMax</strong>). Y is centred on the mean, so five values are negative and the outlier is about 2.2 SDs out (<strong>Standard</strong> - the inflated SD squeezes the others together). Z is centred on the median (half negative, half positive) and the outlier stays at 18 (<strong>Robust</strong>).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>On the unscaled Wisconsin data, chi-square's top 3 are <code>worst area</code>, <code>mean area</code> and <code>area error</code> (scores ≈ 112,600, 54,000 and 8,760). Which two effects explain this ranking?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The three are near-duplicates of the same size information, and chi-square scores each feature on its own, so all three rank highly. Also, scikit-learn's chi2 grows in proportion to a feature's units, which favours large-valued area columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Area causes the tumour to be malignant, so it must rank first.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Chi-square chooses features at random.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Area features have the lowest variance, which chi-square rewards.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Areas are strongly <strong>correlated</strong> with each other (and with radius and perimeter), and a univariate filter doesn't know that the others were already chosen. Also, chi2 is computed from per-class sums of feature values, so multiplying a column by 10 multiplies its score by 10 - areas (hundreds to thousands) beat smoothness (around 0.1). B confuses association with causation. C is false. D is the opposite of what chi-square measures.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A team applies StandardScaler to the 4 x 3 matrix [[1, 2, 0], [1, 5, 1], [1, 8, 0], [1, 11, 1]] and then VarianceThreshold(0.5). Only the constant column is removed, though the binary column had variance 0.25 before scaling. Why?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> VarianceThreshold ignores binary columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> After standardization every non-constant column has variance 1, so a variance threshold no longer measures anything about the original spread - apply it <strong>before</strong> scaling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> StandardScaler sets all variances to 0.5.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The constant column was removed because it is categorical.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Variance is <strong>scale-dependent</strong>. Standardizing forces each non-constant column to variance 1 (a constant column stays at 0), so the threshold only catches constants. A and C are false. D: it was removed because its variance is 0.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A Random Forest gives <code>zip_code</code> the highest importance for predicting loan default. A manager proposes a policy based on \"zip code causing default\". Which assessment is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Accept it - the highest importance proves causation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reject the model - importance scores are meaningless.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Delete zip_code, since important features are suspicious.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Importance only shows the fitted forest relied on zip code to reduce impurity; it may stand in for correlated factors (income, region) and says nothing about cause, so a policy needs separate causal evidence.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Importance means <strong>\"the model used it\"</strong>, not \"it causes the outcome\", and correlated features share or swap importance. A is the core misreading. B throws away a useful diagnostic. C is unjustified.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Protocol 1 selects 15 features on all the data, then runs 5-fold CV: 97%. Protocol 2 runs <code>Pipeline([selector, model])</code> inside the same CV: 94%. Which should be reported?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 94% from Protocol 2 - the selector is refit inside each training fold, so the score reflects how the whole procedure performs on unseen data; Protocol 1 leaked the validation folds into selection.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 97% from Protocol 1 - it is higher.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The average, 95.5%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither - feature selection cannot be evaluated with CV.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Selection is a <strong>learned step</strong>, so it must sit inside the cross-validation loop. Protocol 1's extra 3 points come from choosing features that suit the validation folds. B rewards leakage. C mixes a valid estimate with a biased one. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>You have 20,000 text features, a modest compute budget, and need a model a regulator can read. Which plan is the best trade-off?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RFE with <code>step=1</code> directly on all 20,000 features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> PCA to 50 components.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A cheap filter first (document-frequency thresholds, then chi-square to a few hundred terms), then RFE or model-based selection on that shortlist - all inside cross-validation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Keep all features; the regulator can read 20,000 coefficients.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The lecture's workflow: <strong>filter first for speed, wrapper/embedded second for quality</strong>, validated properly. A needs about 20,000 refits. B creates components that mix all terms, so it loses interpretability. D ignores both cost and readability.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design a leak-free model that tunes the number of chi-square features.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fit MinMaxScaler and SelectKBest on all data; tune k by test accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>Pipeline([(\"scaler\", StandardScaler()), (\"sel\", SelectKBest(chi2)), (\"clf\", LogisticRegression())])</code> tuned on the test set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Choose k from the chi-square scores on the full dataset, then split.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>Pipeline([(\"scaler\", MinMaxScaler()), (\"sel\", SelectKBest(chi2)), (\"clf\", LogisticRegression())])</code> inside <code>GridSearchCV</code> over <code>sel__k</code> on the training set, with a final check on the untouched test set.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>MinMax keeps inputs non-negative for chi2; the pipeline refits scaler and selector on each CV training fold; and the test set is used once, at the end. A and C fit learned steps on all the data. B breaks chi2 with negative values and tunes on the test set.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A dataset feeds a KNN model: <code>income</code> (heavily skewed with real outliers), <code>exam_percent</code> (bounded 0-100), <code>height_cm</code> (roughly bell-shaped) and <code>is_member</code> (0/1). Which preprocessing design is most sensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> MinMaxScaler on everything.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A ColumnTransformer: RobustScaler for income, MinMaxScaler for exam_percent, StandardScaler for height, and is_member left as 0/1 - fitted on training data only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> StandardScaler on income only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No scaling - KNN adapts automatically.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Match the scaler to each <strong>distribution</strong>: robust statistics for outlier-heavy data, MinMax when the bounds are meaningful, standardization for roughly symmetric data, and binary flags already on a 0-1 scale - all fitted after the split. A lets income's outliers compress everyone else. C leaves other features on different scales. D ignores distance dominance.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design feature selection for a spam filter with 50,000 vocabulary terms.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>min_df</code> / <code>max_df</code> to drop very rare and near-universal terms → chi-square to about 2,000 terms → a linear model, with the whole chain in a cross-validated pipeline and the cutoffs tuned there.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RFE with step = 1 on all 50,000 terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> VarianceThreshold(0.5) on the raw counts only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Keep the 100 most frequent words.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Cheap <strong>document-frequency thresholds</strong> remove junk, a <strong>supervised filter</strong> keeps terms associated with spam, and the pipeline keeps everything leak-free. B is far too slow. C ignores the target and is unreliable on sparse counts. D keeps mostly stopwords (\"the\", \"and\"), which carry little signal.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p><code>StandardScaler().fit(X_sparse)</code> on a sparse TF-IDF matrix raises \"Cannot center sparse matrices: pass <code>with_mean=False</code>\". Why?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Centring subtracts the mean from every entry, turning the many zeros into non-zeros and destroying sparsity (and memory); <code>with_mean=False</code> only divides by the SD.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sparse matrices have no mean.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> StandardScaler only works on images.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> TF-IDF values are already standardized.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A 100,000 x 50,000 sparse matrix that is 99.9% zeros would become <strong>dense</strong> after centring. Scaling without centring keeps zeros at zero. B: the mean exists, but subtracting it is the problem. C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>For 1,000 random points in the unit cube, the ratio (nearest distance) / (farthest distance) from a query point was about 0.004 in 2D, 0.23 in 10D, 0.71 in 100D and 0.91 in 1,000D. What does this imply for KNN?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KNN gets more accurate as dimensions are added.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The ratio is a property of the unit cube only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling fixes the effect.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Distances concentrate: in high dimensions the nearest and farthest points are almost equally far, so \"nearest neighbour\" loses meaning unless the data has lower-dimensional structure or irrelevant features are removed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Beyer et al. (1999) showed that for many distributions, relative contrast in distances vanishes as $d$ grows - a precise form of the lecture's <strong>curse of dimensionality</strong> and a strong argument for feature selection before distance-based models. A is the reverse. B: it holds broadly. C: scaling equalizes units but does not restore contrast.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p><code>mutual_info_classif(X, y)</code> gives slightly different scores on two runs unless <code>random_state</code> is fixed. Why?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mutual information is undefined for continuous features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The scores are random guesses.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> For continuous features, scikit-learn estimates MI with a nearest-neighbour method and adds a tiny amount of random noise to break ties, so estimates vary slightly between runs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It refits a Random Forest each time.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>MI must be <strong>estimated</strong> from samples. The k-NN (Kraskov-style) estimator plus jitter makes it slightly stochastic, and it is noisy on small samples. True MI is ≥ 0 and is 0 only under independence, which is why it can detect the nonlinear dependence that correlation misses. A, B and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p><code>QuantileTransformer(n_quantiles=6)</code> (uniform output) is fitted on the views [1, 5, 25, 300, 1295, 19000]. What are the outputs?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0, 0.0002, 0.0013, 0.016, 0.068, 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> -0.16, -0.15, -0.13, 0.13, 1.09, 18.18</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All 0.5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A quantile transform maps each value to its <strong>rank-based position</strong> in the training distribution, so equal steps in rank become equal steps in output and the outlier's size no longer matters. A is MinMax. C is RobustScaler. It is a non-linear option beyond the lecture's three linear scalers - useful for heavy tails, but it distorts the distances between values.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A Lasso regression ($L_1$ penalty $\\alpha\\sum|w_j|$) is fitted on 10 features where only $x_1$ and $x_2$ matter. As $\\alpha$ increases from 0.01 to 2, what happens to the coefficients?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All coefficients shrink equally but none reaches zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The coefficients grow.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> More and more coefficients become <strong>exactly zero</strong> - first the irrelevant ones, eventually even $x_2$ - so Lasso performs embedded feature selection.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The coefficients are unaffected; α only changes speed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The $L_1$ penalty's corners push weak coefficients to <strong>exactly 0</strong>, which is selection during training (embedded, like forest importance). A describes the $L_2$ (Ridge) penalty, which shrinks but rarely zeroes. In a quick check, α = 0.5 kept only $x_1, x_2$, and α = 2 kept only $x_1$. Lasso needs scaled features so the penalty treats them fairly.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>The Boruta method adds a \"shadow\" copy of every feature with its values shuffled, fits a Random Forest, and repeats this several times. What is the decision rule?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Keep every feature, shadows included.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Keep only the shadow features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Keep features whose importance repeatedly beats the <strong>best shadow feature's</strong> importance; reject those that consistently do not.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Keep features whose importance is above the average real feature.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Shuffled shadows have no relationship with the target, so the best shadow sets a <strong>data-driven noise floor</strong>. It is a wrapper built around embedded importance, which turns the lecture's RF importance into a statistical test. D compares against the wrong baseline. A and B are meaningless.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A skewed feature contains zeros and negative values (e.g. account balance). Which power transform can be applied directly?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Box-Cox, because it handles any real number.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Neither - power transforms require strictly positive data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A log transform, $\\log(x)$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Yeo-Johnson (scikit-learn's default <code>PowerTransformer</code>), because it is defined for zero and negative values; Box-Cox requires strictly positive data.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><code>PowerTransformer(method=\"box-cox\")</code> raises \"can only be applied to strictly positive data\"; Yeo-Johnson extends the idea to all reals and, by default, standardizes the result. C fails at 0 and below. Like RobustScaler, it addresses skew and outliers, but by changing the <strong>shape</strong> of the distribution rather than just shifting and stretching it.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A random <code>customer_id</code> column (unique per row) gets a high impurity-based importance in a Random Forest, yet permutation importance on a validation set is about 0. What explains this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Customer IDs are genuinely predictive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Impurity importance is computed on training data and favours high-cardinality features that offer many split points to memorize; permutation importance on held-out data shows the feature does not help generalization.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Permutation importance is always zero for numeric features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The forest was trained without bootstrapping.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This is a known <strong>bias of impurity (MDI) importance</strong>: many unique values let trees carve out training rows. Measuring the accuracy drop when a feature is shuffled on validation data avoids it. It refines the lecture's \"importance ≠ causation\" point: importance can also be inflated by noise. A, C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>With 50 rows, 5,000 pure-noise features and random labels, selecting the top 20 features on <strong>all</strong> rows and then running 5-fold CV gives about 96% accuracy. Putting the selector inside the CV pipeline gives about 52%. What does this demonstrate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Selection bias: choosing features using the validation rows lets noise that happens to match their labels into the model, so CV becomes wildly optimistic even when no signal exists.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The pipeline version has a bug.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Noise features are informative in small samples.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 5-fold CV is unreliable in general.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Ambroise and McLachlan (2002) described exactly this on gene-expression data. With $p\\gg n$, some noise features correlate with the labels by chance; picking them on all rows <strong>leaks</strong> the folds. The honest pipeline returns chance level (~50%). It is the lecture's leakage rule, shown at its most extreme. B, C and D draw the wrong conclusions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>In <code>GridSearchCV(pipe, {\"sel__k\": [5, 10, 15]}, cv=5)</code>, where <code>pipe</code> contains a selector named <code>\"sel\"</code>, which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The selector is fitted once on all the data, and only the model is cross-validated.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>sel__k</code> is a typo - parameter names cannot contain double underscores.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The grid tests k on the test set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> For each k and each fold, the entire pipeline (scaler, selector, model) is refit on the training part of the fold, so k is chosen without leakage.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><code>step__param</code> addresses a parameter of a named pipeline step, and cross-validation clones and refits the <strong>whole pipeline</strong> per fold. That is how the lecture's \"fit preprocessing on training data only\" rule scales up to hyperparameter search. A is the leaky alternative. B and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A sensor company must cut 200 sensor readings down to about 20 for a cheaper product. PCA to 20 components and selecting 20 original sensors give similar validation accuracy. Which should be shipped?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> PCA - components are always better.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Selection - the product only needs the 20 chosen sensors, whereas every PCA component is a mix of all 200 readings, so all 200 sensors would still have to be installed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Either - they are equivalent.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither - the accuracy is the same as with 200 sensors.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture's distinction: <strong>selection keeps original variables; extraction builds new ones</strong>. When the cost is measuring the raw features, only selection cuts it, and it stays interpretable. A over-generalizes. C ignores deployment cost. D misreads the goal.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Stability selection runs a selector (e.g. Lasso) on many random half-samples and keeps features chosen in, say, more than 70% of runs. What does it offer beyond a single selection?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A guarantee that the selected features cause the outcome.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Robustness: features that appear only due to the quirks of one sample are filtered out, and Meinshausen and Bühlmann (2010) give a bound on the expected number of false selections.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Faster training than one Lasso fit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It always selects more features.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A single run, especially with correlated features (as in Wisconsin), can be <strong>unstable</strong> - small data changes swap the chosen twins. Selection frequencies across subsamples measure reliability and come with error control. A: stability is not causality. C: it is slower (many fits). D: it usually selects fewer.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
